-When the output goes to a file or a pipe, the script prints a plain line instead, every `BEAT` seconds (30). `--progress bar|lines|off` (`PROGRESS` in Spyder) overrides that choice; `off` prints only each stage's start and end. The tools' own output goes only to the logs in `<id>_build/logs/`, one per step. When a step fails, the script prints the last 40 lines of that step's log and the log's path.
+When the output goes to a file or a pipe, the script prints a plain line instead, every `BEAT` seconds (30). `--progress bar|lines|off` (`PROGRESS` in Spyder) overrides that choice; `off` prints only each stage's start and end. The tools' own output goes only to the logs in `<id>_build/logs/`, one per step. When a step fails, the script sends the last 40 lines of that step's log and the log's path through `say`.
+
+For machine consumers, `--progress json` writes one UTF-8 JSON object per stdout line. The event vocabulary is `stage` (with `k`, `n`, `name`, `step`), `step`, `progress`, `stage_end` (including `ok`, `summary`, `elapsed`), and `say` (`msg`). Progress snapshots include `done`, `total`, `unit`, `est`, `note`, `frac`, `eta`, and `elapsed`; unknown totals, fractions, and ETAs are JSON `null`, never guessed. Count-only changes are limited to one event per 0.5 seconds, while stage/step changes and progress metadata changes are emitted promptly; the final count is flushed before `stage_end`. Messages are JSON-escaped (so embedded newlines remain inside one record), Unicode is preserved, and all script diagnostics including build failures go through `say`. Child process output remains in per-step logs; on a child failure its log tail is a `say` event. This mode does not prompt for missing paths: supply `nsp_dir` and `-k/--keys` on the command line. Argument-parser errors remain on stderr, and `--help` remains ordinary human-readable text. The existing `auto`, `bar`, `lines`, and `off` modes keep their human-readable behavior.
+
+Run the isolated progress tests without game files, keys, Xcode, or build dependencies:
+
+```sh
+python3 -B -W error -m unittest discover -s scripts/tests -p 'test_*.py' -v
+```
 
 Everything it writes stays in the NSP folder: intermediate files and logs in `<id>_build/`, the IPA next to the NSPs. The extracted game files stay in `<id>_build/extract/` for the next build; `--no-keep-extracted` (`KEEP_EXTRACTED = False` in Spyder) deletes them at the end. It exits with 0 when done and 1 on an error. The app's bundle ID is `org.nxnative.<base game title ID>` (for example `org.nxnative.0100a6301214e000`), and its version is the game's display version: the update's when an update is packed, else the base game's (Engage 2.0.0); `--app-name` / `--bundle-id` (or `APP_NAME` / `BUNDLE_ID` in Spyder) override the name and ID.
 The game language (`--language`, `LANGUAGE`; English by default) is what `nn::oe::GetDesiredLanguage` returns. As on the console, it must be one the game's control data lists (`SupportedLanguageFlag`, `nacp.py --languages`); the packed version's control data counts, since an update may add languages. The same language for another region stands in (`en-GB` for `en-US`, `es` for `es-419`, `fr` for `fr-CA`, `pt` for `pt-BR`, and back). A language the game lacks stops the build with the game's list; English, the default, falls back to the game's first language.
